,"parts":[{"text":"மடிந்த","lemma":"மடி","gloss":"having died"}],"gloss":"having died"},{"surface":"தொல்சுவர்","parts":[{"text":"தொல்சுவர்","lemma":"தொல்சுவர்","gloss":"old wall"}],"gloss":"old wall"},{"surface":"வரைப்பின்","parts":[{"text":"வரைப்பின்","lemma":"வரைப்பு","gloss":"of the house"}],"gloss":"of the house"}]},{"words":[{"surface":"பாஅல்","parts":[{"text":"பாஅல்","lemma":"பால்","gloss":"milk"}],"gloss":"milk"},{"surface":"இன்மையின்","parts":[{"text":"இன்மையின்","lemma":"இன்மை","gloss":"because of the lack"}],"gloss":"because of the lack"},{"surface":"பல்பாடு","parts":[{"text":"பல்பாடு","lemma":"பல்பாடு","gloss":"many times"}],"gloss":"many times"},{"surface":"சுவைத்து","parts":[{"text":"சுவைத்து","lemma":"சுவை","gloss":"having sucked"}],"gloss":"having sucked"}]},{"words":[{"surface":"முலைக்கோள்","parts":[{"text":"முலைக்கோள்","lemma":"முலைக்கோள்","gloss":"breastfeeding"}],"gloss":"breastfeeding"},{"surface":"மறந்த","parts":[{"text":"மறந்த","lemma":"மற","gloss":"having forgotten"}],"gloss":"having forgotten"},{"surface":"புதல்வனொடு","parts":[{"text":"புதல்வனொடு","lemma":"புதல்வன்","gloss":"with the son"}],"gloss":"with the son"}]},{"words":[{"surface":"மனைத்","parts":[{"text":"மனைத்","lemma":"மனை","gloss":"house"}],"gloss":"house"},{"surface":"தொலைந்திருந்","parts":[{"text":"தொலைந்திருந்","lemma":"தொலைந்திரு","gloss":"having been ruined"}],"gloss":"having been ruined"},{"surface":"தவென்வாள்","parts":[{"text":"தவென்வாள்","lemma":"என்வாள்","gloss":"my bright"}],"gloss":"my bright"},{"surface":"நுதற்","parts":[{"text":"நுதற்","lemma":"நுதல்","gloss":"forehead"}],"gloss":"forehead"},{"surface":"படர்ந்தே","parts":[{"text":"படர்ந்தே","lemma":"படருதல்","gloss":"thinking of"}],"gloss":"thinking of"}]}],"literal":"Like the roar that strikes to shatter the hills, making the tall mountain shine as if standing to see, severing the precious head of the snake with terror, of the nature of inspiring fear, the thunderbolt of a fierce angry storm / having gone to the front with the army sounding with the rising drum, conquering so that kings fall, O lord of abundant fame! / I, the seeker of gifts of high status who came thinking of you, / saying 'He is generous, therefore I will bow to this man', / while unreceptive people speak of your meanness, / you indeed completed what you thought in the past days! / Having shown it as if it is inside the hand, and on the next day, standing outside with distress and lies, / even though you do not feel ashamed, having spoken to make you feel ashamed, my refined excellent tongue suffering, having praised, / singing and singing, having worshipped and praised your victorious wide chest that has won sung fame, / I will go indeed, thinking of my bright-foreheaded wife who was ruined at home / with the son who forgot breastfeeding, having sucked many times because of the lack of milk, / in the house of old walls where house rats have died, wandering from place to place because of the lack of food every day.","translation":"Like the thunderbolt of a fierce, angry storm that shatters the hills and severs the head of the venomous snake, making the tall mountains tremble, you march with roaring drums and conquering armies to defeat rival kings, O lord of abundant fame! I came to you seeking gifts, thinking, 'He is generous, so I shall bow to him.' But while others spoke of your reluctance, you achieved what you intended. After showing me hope as if the gift were already in my hand, you made me wait outside in distress day after day with false promises. Though you felt no shame, I spoke to make you feel ashamed, praising you until my refined tongue grew weary. Singing of your glory and worshipping your victorious, wide chest, I now depart. I go back to my bright-foreheaded wife, who sits ruined in our house of crumbling walls where even the rats have died of hunger, and where our little son, wandering from place to place in search of food, has forgotten how to suckle because his mother's breasts have run dry.","modern":"அச்சம் தரும் தன்மையுடைய, சினங்கொண்ட இடியுடன் கூடிய மேகம், நின்றுகாணும் படியாக நீண்ட மலைகள் அதிர, பாம்பின் தலையைத் துண்டித்து, குன்றுகளைத் தகர்க்கும் பேரோசையைப் போல, முரசுகள் முழங்கப் போர்க்களத்திற்குச் சென்று பகை அரசர்களை வெல்லும் புகழ்மிக்க தலைவனே! உன்னை வள்ளல் என்று எண்ணி, உன்னை வணங்கிப் பரிசு பெற வந்தேன். ஆனால், உன்னுடைய ஈகைக் குணத்தைப் பற்றிப் பிறர் குறை கூறும்படி, நீ முன்பு நினைத்ததைச் செய்து முடித்தாய். கையில் பொருள் இருப்பது போலக் காட்டி, மறுநாளில் பொய் கூறி என்னை வெளியில் காக்க வைத்து வருத்தினாய். நீ வெட்கப்படாவிட்டாலும், உன்னை வெட்கமடையச் செய்யும் வகையில் என் நாவினால் உன்னைப் புகழ்ந்து பாடினேன். உன்னுடைய வெற்றியைத் தரும் மார்பினை வணங்கிப் போற்றிவிட்டு நான் விடைபெறுகிறேன். உணவின்றி வறுமையால் வாடி, எலிகளும் மடிந்துபோன பழைய சுவர்களைக் கொண்ட வீட்டில், பால் இல்லாததால் தாய்ப்பால் குடிப்பதை மறந்து தவிக்கும் என் மகனுடனும், வாடிய முகத்துடனும் இருக்கும் என் மனைவியை நினைத்து நான் என் இல்லத்திற்குத் திரும்புகிறேன்.","explanation":"The poet Peruṅkuṉṟūr Kiḻār addresses the Cera king Ceramāṉ Kuṭakkōc Cēral Irumpoṟai in this poem of the 'pari cil kaṭānilai' theme (demanding or departing without gifts). The poet describes the king's fierce military might, comparing his army's march to a terrifying thunderbolt that shatters hills and kills snakes. However, the poet contrasts this grand power with the king's disappointing behavior in delaying and denying the promised gifts, making the poet wait outside in humiliation. The poet vividly depicts the extreme poverty of his home, where the walls are crumbling, even the house rats have died of starvation, and his infant son has forgotten how to suckle because his mother's breasts have dried up due to hunger. Ultimately, the poet decides to leave without the gift, choosing to return to his suffering family rather than endure further empty promises.","model":"gemini-3.5-flash"}}